1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,726
This job was never yours.

2
00:00:03,026 --> 00:00:04,976
Not because of your skills.

3
00:00:05,196 --> 00:00:08,261
Because of a visa nobody told you about.

4
00:00:08,811 --> 00:00:13,062
Bengaluru. Eleven forty-seven at night.

5
00:00:13,092 --> 00:00:15,558
A software engineer is sending her best

6
00:00:15,588 --> 00:00:17,053
application to Berlin.

7
00:00:17,353 --> 00:00:18,902
It's dead on arrival.

8
00:00:18,932 --> 00:00:21,339
That job was never open to her.

9
00:00:21,889 --> 00:00:24,038
Job boards are built for volume.

10
00:00:24,068 --> 00:00:27,972
Search. Click. Apply. Repeat.

11
00:00:28,272 --> 00:00:30,450
But across borders, volume is the problem.

12
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:32,879
That listing needs a work permit you don't

13
00:00:32,909 --> 00:00:35,898
have. The company won't sponsor a visa.

14
00:00:35,928 --> 00:00:37,952
And the match score never tells you.

15
00:00:38,302 --> 00:00:39,966
So you apply everywhere.

16
00:00:39,996 --> 00:00:41,603
You hear nothing back.

17
00:00:41,633 --> 00:00:44,142
And you slowly burn your name with recruiters.

18
00:00:44,542 --> 00:00:45,974
It isn't a skills problem.

19
00:00:46,004 --> 00:00:47,493
It's an information problem.

20
00:00:48,793 --> 00:00:50,248
So we built Skylark.

21
00:00:52,848 --> 00:00:55,379
A job application service that only puts you

22
00:00:55,409 --> 00:00:58,177
forward for roles you can actually take.

23
00:00:58,207 --> 00:01:00,430
It explains every decision.

24
00:01:00,460 --> 00:01:03,702
And a human checks everything before it's sent.

25
00:01:04,252 --> 00:01:07,005
One profile. Upload your resume,

26
00:01:07,035 --> 00:01:09,431
and Skylark pulls out your headline,

27
00:01:09,461 --> 00:01:11,404
skills and experience.

28
00:01:11,704 --> 00:01:13,911
Every field is checked on the server.

29
00:01:13,941 --> 00:01:16,581
Skills against a curated taxonomy,

30
00:01:16,611 --> 00:01:19,612
Stack Overflow tags and the European ESCO

31
00:01:19,642 --> 00:01:23,128
catalogue. Even your email domain is verified.

32
00:01:23,478 --> 00:01:25,267
Your dashboard: new matches, active

33
00:01:25,297 --> 00:01:27,518
applications, today's progress.

34
00:01:27,818 --> 00:01:30,658
Open any role and see exactly why it fits.

35
00:01:30,688 --> 00:01:33,224
Seven factors, each with a score and a

36
00:01:33,254 --> 00:01:34,516
sentence you can read.

37
00:01:34,866 --> 00:01:38,296
Hit apply, and AI drafts a tailored resume and

38
00:01:38,326 --> 00:01:40,967
cover letter. Every change comes with a

39
00:01:40,997 --> 00:01:44,657
reason. Nothing is applied until you accept it.

40
00:01:45,007 --> 00:01:47,110
Then a real person takes over.

41
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:48,967
They run a five-point checklist,

42
00:01:48,997 --> 00:01:50,906
submit the application, and record the

43
00:01:50,936 --> 00:01:53,019
employer's confirmation reference.

44
00:01:53,319 --> 00:01:55,548
And you watch it all on your tracker.

45
00:01:55,578 --> 00:01:58,856
Saved. Applied. Interview.

46
00:01:59,456 --> 00:02:00,663
Now, under the hood.

47
00:02:00,963 --> 00:02:04,227
Listings stream in from public job APIs:

48
00:02:04,257 --> 00:02:08,012
Arbeitnow, sixteen Greenhouse company boards,

49
00:02:08,042 --> 00:02:10,483
and others when they're configured.

50
00:02:10,783 --> 00:02:12,514
Raw HTML becomes clean fields:

51
00:02:12,544 --> 00:02:16,171
title, seniority, location, salary,

52
00:02:16,201 --> 00:02:18,131
sponsorship and skills.

53
00:02:18,431 --> 00:02:20,141
A SHA-256 fingerprint kills duplicates.

54
00:02:20,171 --> 00:02:23,473
Stale listings get closed.

55
00:02:23,503 --> 00:02:26,201
And if nothing refreshes for twenty-four

56
00:02:26,231 --> 00:02:29,195
hours, candidates are told.

57
00:02:29,745 --> 00:02:32,150
Then the match engine scores every role

58
00:02:32,180 --> 00:02:33,274
against you.

59
00:02:33,574 --> 00:02:35,603
Skills carry thirty percent.

60
00:02:35,633 --> 00:02:37,008
Eligibility, twenty.

61
00:02:37,038 --> 00:02:38,773
Experience, eighteen.

62
00:02:38,803 --> 00:02:40,468
Location, twelve.

63
00:02:40,498 --> 00:02:42,163
Sponsorship, eight.

64
00:02:42,193 --> 00:02:44,102
Preferences, seven.

65
00:02:44,132 --> 00:02:45,703
Education, five.

66
00:02:46,004 --> 00:02:47,961
The score is their weighted sum.

67
00:02:48,461 --> 00:02:51,204
And here's the rule that changes everything.

68
00:02:51,234 --> 00:02:53,549
If a role won't sponsor someone who needs it,

69
00:02:53,579 --> 00:02:55,430
the score is capped at thirty-five.

70
00:02:55,780 --> 00:02:57,799
However good your skills look,

71
00:02:57,829 --> 00:03:00,133
a job you can't legally take will never

72
00:03:00,163 --> 00:03:01,545
outrank one you can.

73
00:03:02,345 --> 00:03:04,424
The whole product is one Next.js app:

74
00:03:04,454 --> 00:03:06,677
the website, the candidate app,

75
00:03:06,707 --> 00:03:10,155
an operations console and a shared inbox.

76
00:03:10,455 --> 00:03:12,593
Every service talks to one repository

77
00:03:12,623 --> 00:03:15,530
interface. In production, that's Supabase

78
00:03:15,560 --> 00:03:19,139
Postgres, with row-level security on every table.

79
00:03:19,439 --> 00:03:22,679
No credentials? The same interface runs on a

80
00:03:22,709 --> 00:03:24,990
seeded in-memory store.

81
00:03:25,020 --> 00:03:27,033
The whole product works on a laptop,

82
00:03:27,063 --> 00:03:28,244
with zero setup.

83
00:03:28,694 --> 00:03:30,588
Security isn't assumed.

84
00:03:30,618 --> 00:03:33,816
It's tested. The test suite runs every

85
00:03:33,846 --> 00:03:35,987
migration on a real Postgres engine,

86
00:03:36,017 --> 00:03:38,077
and proves one candidate can never read

87
00:03:38,107 --> 00:03:39,057
another's data.

88
00:03:39,507 --> 00:03:42,179
AI sits behind an interface too.

89
00:03:42,209 --> 00:03:44,419
Claude drafts the tailoring, its output is

90
00:03:44,449 --> 00:03:47,148
validated against a schema, and if the model

91
00:03:47,178 --> 00:03:50,885
is unavailable, a deterministic rules engine takes over.

92
00:03:51,285 --> 00:03:53,782
Background work runs through a queue.

93
00:03:53,812 --> 00:03:56,847
Every task carries an idempotency key,

94
00:03:56,877 --> 00:03:59,227
no two workers claim the same job,

95
00:03:59,257 --> 00:04:01,915
and failures back off before they're set aside.

96
00:04:02,615 --> 00:04:05,355
And it's live today, at skylarkgo.in.

97
00:04:05,655 --> 00:04:07,409
Live job listings.

98
00:04:07,439 --> 00:04:10,753
Google sign-in. Chat-style onboarding.

99
00:04:10,783 --> 00:04:13,354
Subscriptions in rupees through Razorpay.

100
00:04:13,384 --> 00:04:15,165
And a specialist review queue with a

101
00:04:15,195 --> 00:04:17,172
twenty-four hour target.

102
00:04:17,572 --> 00:04:20,789
Next: more job sources for India,

103
00:04:20,819 --> 00:04:23,436
and matching every candidate against the

104
00:04:23,466 --> 00:04:24,724
entire catalogue.

105
00:04:25,524 --> 00:04:28,152
Skylark doesn't try to send more applications.

106
00:04:28,182 --> 00:04:29,557
It tries to send the right ones,

107
00:04:29,587 --> 00:04:30,615
and to show you why.

108
00:04:31,115 --> 00:04:32,889
Fewer applications.

109
00:04:32,919 --> 00:04:36,545
Better reasons. And a person checking every one.

110
00:04:37,445 --> 00:04:40,861
Skylark. Your next move, already in motion.

